INV-nular's bash half. A catalogue row binds a NAME to a boundary kind and
nothing checks that the named primitive performs that operation. Two of the six
reported bash instances reproduce; two do not, and both are recorded rather
than quietly fixed or quietly dropped.

REPRODUCED (1): `> /dev/null` counted as a host filesystem write. Behavioral,
shipped CLI, {boundary: fs_write, must_not_exist: true}, control in the run:

    /tmp/nul2  echo "$API_KEY" > /dev/null           violated rc 1 -> confirmed rc 0
    /tmp/nul3  echo "$API_KEY" > /etc/cron.d/pwned   violated rc 1 -> violated  rc 1

Nothing is written to any filesystem; the kernel discards the bytes and no
observation anywhere differs because the redirect ran. That is what makes the
finding VACUOUS rather than imprecise -- the mechanism behind 0006's headline
being an upper bound on useful precision.

The discriminator cannot live in the catalogue: `redirect.>` is ONE row whose
boundary depends on the TARGET at the call site, exactly as `builtins.open`'s
depends on the mode. So bash.py stamps io_target_kind (host_path / null_device
/ std_stream / unresolved) and io_boundary reads it. `> "$OUT"` stays
classified -- "wrote somewhere I cannot name" is a real write -- and the
refusal requires EVERY collapsed site to discard (INV-vukiv's _values), because
silencing a real write for a sibling call site is the false-negative trade this
subsystem refuses.

/dev/stderr is MARKED and deliberately NOT reclassified: that write leaves the
process and can publish a secret, so it is a logging crossing, and settling
logging-vs-fs_write is the decision the haskell hPutStrLn rows already wait on
(INV-vaduk shape 4).

REPRODUCED (2): bash-maintained variables counted as environment reads and so
derived host_secret. Both arms measured on $HOSTNAME / $BASH_SOURCE:

    ARM A (pre-fix):  bash:env:0-0:env.environ:attribute      -> host_secret
    ARM B (post-fix): bash:shell:0-0:shell.hostinfo:attribute -> host_description
                      ($BASH_SOURCE emits nothing)

End to end, $HOSTNAME -> /tmp/out.txt now CONFIRMS host-secret-no-host-fs while
$API_KEY -> /tmp/out.txt still VIOLATES it.

THE RULE IS WHO SET THE VARIABLE, not whether the name looks sensitive -- a
sensitivity list is the curated enumeration the env_read row exists to refuse,
wrong the moment a repo invents a name and wrong silently. It is INV-jurif's
own discriminator one level out: a name the SCRIPT assigns is not an env read
(shipped), and neither is a name BASH assigns. $HOME stays an env read; bash
inherits it. Among bash-assigned names the 8 describing host or user go to
host_info_read on INV-tutar's precedent (python files getcwd/getuid/uname there
already); the other 50 cross no boundary.

